module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.math.bigint.{BigInt, cmp as big_cmp, parse as big_parse, zero as big_zero}

// What the compiler emits and the machine runs.
//
// **The tree-walker is being replaced because of `await`, and for no other reason.** A callback runs
// from an event loop, never from the middle of an expression, so callbacks and promises need nothing
// new. `await` has to suspend *inside* an expression, and a tree-walker's state is the sysl call
// stack, which cannot be captured. So the state moves onto a heap of slate's own, where a suspension
// is nothing more grand than declining to run a frame for a while.
//
// **The collector gets simpler, which was not the reason but is the larger gain.** `alloc` never
// collects, so a value held in a sysl local is invisible to the mark phase -- which is why the
// tree-walker carries a shadow stack of `hold`/`release` pairs, and why a missed one shows up as a
// rare crash rather than a failing test. A machine keeps its values in an operand stack that is an
// ordinary array, so the tracer walks it and the whole class of mistake goes away.
//
// **What is deliberately NOT compiled is a pattern.** `match_pattern` already exists, is tested, and
// is not on any hot path, so an arm's pattern is one instruction carrying an index into a table. The
// same goes for arithmetic and for calling: `binary` and `apply` are called by the machine exactly as
// the walker called them. A rewrite that reimplements settled semantics is a rewrite that changes
// them by accident.

// One instruction.
//
// **The operand stack is where everything happens**, so most of these say only what to do; the values
// they do it to are already on top.
enum Op
    // -- constants --
    //
    // **THE THREE A LOOP NEVER PUSHES ARE AT THE END OF THIS GROUP AND NOT THE START, and the
    // dispatch is why.** A jump table is built over a RANGE of tags, so the lowest tag the hot
    // instructions hold is where the table begins -- and a cold variant below all of them costs a
    // subtract on every instruction the machine executes. `PushInt` holds tag zero for that reason.
    PushInt(v: long)
    PushReal(v: real)

    // The text lives in the unit's string table, so an instruction stays a fixed-size thing with no
    // reference-counted member in it.
    PushStr(k: usize)

    // A whole literal too wide for 64 bits. **The NUMBER lives in the unit's table and not the
    // digits**, which is where this differs from `PushStr`: reading text into a magnitude is a
    // division per chunk of nine digits, and a literal inside a loop would pay it every turn. The
    // limbs are built once when the literal is interned, and the instruction copies a view.
    PushBig(k: usize)

    // The value a `type` declaration binds -- the pattern at `pk` and the name at `k`, both indices
    // into the unit, so this stays a fixed-size instruction like every other constant.
    //
    // **It is an instruction rather than a constant folded in at compile time because a shape
    // captures the scope it was declared in**, which only exists while the module runs.
    PushShape(pk: u32, k: u32)

    // What an `external` declaration binds -- the interned path, which IS the value.
    //
    // **The interpreter has no host to read the path from, and the instruction still runs.** It
    // makes the value and every operation on it faults naming the command, so a module that binds a
    // browser API can be imported by a program that never reaches for one.
    PushExternal(k: usize)

    // The three a compiled loop never reaches: `null` and `undefined` are written where a statement
    // answers nothing, and a boolean literal is folded into the jump that tests it.
    PushNull
    PushUndefined
    PushBool(v: bool)

    // -- names --
    //
    // **Names are still looked up in the scope chain rather than in numbered slots.** Slot-indexing
    // locals is the obvious next optimisation and is deliberately not done here: this rewrite has to
    // preserve behaviour exactly, and resolving names at compile time changes when a mistake is
    // reported.
    LoadName(k: usize)

    // A read the compiler resolved to one of the file's own top-level definitions: push what the
    // machine's table holds at `d`, which is where the hoisted definition put its closure.
    //
    // **It is an index and not a pointer for the reason `PushModule` carries one**: the value belongs
    // to the machine that ran the file, and an actor runs the file on a machine of its own.
    //
    // **What makes it sound is stated in `defs.sysl`** -- a definition written at the file's own top
    // level whose spelling nothing else in the file binds or writes. A spelling anything else binds
    // keeps `LoadName` and the outward walk, so nothing a program can observe is different.
    LoadDef(d: usize)

    // The same table, for a `val` or a `var` written at the file's own top level rather than a
    // definition. **The difference from `LoadDef` is that the cell may not be filled yet**: a
    // definition is hoisted, so its cell holds a closure before the first statement runs, while a
    // `var` is bound when its own statement is reached. An empty cell is `Undefined`, which a
    // program may never keep -- `keepable` refuses to bind or store one -- so it says "nothing has
    // been put here" and nothing else.
    //
    // **An empty cell falls back to the lookup by spelling**, which is what makes this safe rather
    // than merely fast: a read that runs before the binding does says exactly what it always said,
    // and a VM that has run only a file's declarations reads nothing out of a cell at all.
    LoadCell(d: u32, k: u32)

    // `val name = v` / `var name = v` at the file's own top level, where the compiler gave the name
    // a cell: the scope is bound exactly as `DeclareVal`/`DeclareVar` bind it, and the cell is
    // filled beside it.
    //
    // `Unit.wide`: `a` the cell, `b` the name.
    DeclareCell(w: u32, mutable: bool)

    // `name = v` where `name` is a `var` at the file's own top level: the cell takes the value and
    // the module's scope takes it too.
    //
    // **THE SCOPE IS WRITTEN THROUGH ON EVERY STORE, AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT.** The cell is the
    // fast path and the scope is still the only one an `import`'s load per export, an actor looking
    // a class up by name, or any read the compiler could not resolve will take -- so the two are
    // kept saying the same thing. What the cell saves is the walk and the two extra hashes an
    // assignment costs: `assign_name` asks whether each scope has the name, asks whether it is
    // writable, and then puts. Here the compiler has already answered both.
    StoreCell(d: u32, k: u32)

    DeclareVal(k: usize)
    DeclareVar(k: usize)
    StoreName(k: usize)

    // A parameter nobody gave, bound to `undefined` rather than left unbound. **Not `DeclareVar`**,
    // which refuses to store `undefined` -- a call leaving a parameter out is not a program storing
    // absence, it is the absence a missing field already answers with, so the guarded head that emits
    // this is exempt from that refusal on purpose. `compile.sysl`'s `compile_required_absences`.
    DeclareAbsent(k: usize)

    // -- names the compiler resolved --
    //
    // **A slot is a cell of the OPERAND STACK, below the frame's working area.** A chunk that wants
    // any reserves them at entry, so `base` is where they start and `Ret`'s truncate back to `base`
    // is what frees them. Three things come out of that and each is a reason it is done this way:
    // the collector already walks the stack, so a slot is traced without the mark phase learning
    // about a new kind of thing; a coroutine and a generator run on a machine with a stack of their
    // own, so their slots suspend with them and nothing writes them down; and a `Handler` already
    // records a stack height to cut back to.
    //
    // **A name gets a slot only where NOTHING OUTSIDE THE CHUNK CAN SEE IT.** A closure captures the
    // scope chain by pointer, so an enclosing frame and a closure made inside it share the very same
    // `EnvObj` and see each other's writes -- which is what a cell would have to provide, already
    // provided. So a chunk holding any nested function keeps every name in the chain, and a module's
    // names are never slotted at all: an import is a load per export, so they are read by name from
    // outside.
    //
    // **A name the compiler cannot resolve keeps `LoadName` and faults at run time exactly as
    // before**, so nothing is refused earlier than it was and the checker's rule -- report only what
    // the machine would also refuse -- is untouched.
    LoadSlot(i: usize)
    DeclareSlot(i: usize)
    StoreSlot(i: usize)

    // A write to a slot holding a `val`. **The checker refuses this before the machine ever runs**,
    // and it is emitted anyway so that the one path where it does not -- a write the checker could
    // not see -- says the sentence it has always said rather than "not defined".
    ImmutableSlot(k: usize)

    // `val n: integer = v` where `n` is a slot. **The annotation is checked where the value arrives**
    // exactly as `CheckType` checks it, and the only difference is where the value is read from --
    // which is the whole of what a slot changes.
    //
    // **The slot is inline and the rest of it is in `Unit.wide`** -- `a` the name, `b` the type it
    // was declared as, `c` the pattern that stands for it.
    CheckSlot(i: u32, w: u32)

    // Collect if enough has been allocated to be worth it, and report a heap that is full.
    //
    // **Safe at any instruction boundary, which the walker's collection point was not.** Everything
    // live is on the operand stack, in a frame, or in a scope, and the tracer walks all three.
    //
    // **Emitted in front of every statement that is not in a loop's body, and once at the top of
    // every loop** -- a statement in a loop being the only kind that runs more than once for having
    // been written once. See `safe_point` in `emit.sysl`.
    Tick

    // -- FUSED PAIRS: ONE DISPATCH WHERE THE EMITTER WROTE TWO --
    //
    // **THEY ARE DECLARED HERE, AMONG THE SLOT INSTRUCTIONS THEY FUSE, RATHER THAN AT THE END WHERE
    // THEY WERE ADDED.** A variant's position in this enum IS its tag, and the dispatch's first jump
    // table is built only where the tags it spans are dense enough -- so the instructions the loop
    // actually executes have to hold near-neighbouring tags. Declared last, these five held 105
    // through 109 and every one of them fell through to the dispatch's second table.
    // `bench/results/2026-09-22-one-jump-table.md` has the measurement, and the head of the `match`
    // in `run_frames.sysl` says what the other half of the rule is.
    //
    // **The instruction FETCH is what these remove, and the fetch is 12.4% of all sampled time.**
    // `Buf.at<Ins>` is an out-of-line call that retains the code buffer, bounds-checks it twice and
    // copies 56 bytes, once per instruction executed;
    // `bench/results/2026-09-21-sampled-profile.md`'s sampled profile reads it out of the shipped
    // binary. A pair of instructions run as one pays that once instead of twice,
    // and drops the push-then-pop of the value the first was only ever computing for the second.
    // CPython's `LOAD_FAST_LOAD_FAST` is the same move, chosen the same way.
    //
    // **WHICH FOUR: the profiler's own pair table over `arith`, `loops`, `fib`, `nested`, `calls`,
    // `methods` and `dispatch` picked them**, and they are the four heaviest sequences that are also
    // broad -- `LoadSlot`+`PushInt` 82.8M, `LoadSlot`+`LoadSlot` 55.0M, `Add`+`StoreSlot` 40.0M,
    // `Less`+`JumpIfFalse` 31.4M. Together they take a loop's condition from four instructions to
    // two and its counter's increment from four to two. `profile.sysl`'s `pairs` table is where that
    // measurement comes from and is the thing to re-run before adding a fifth.
    //
    // **A FUSION IS LEGAL WHATEVER JUMPS AT IT, AND THE REASON IS THAT THE SECOND INSTRUCTION IS
    // LEFT WHERE IT WAS.** `fuse` in `emit.sysl` overwrites only the FIRST slot of a pair and the
    // second stays in the code buffer untouched, so a jump, a handler or a loop head that targets it
    // runs the very instruction the emitter wrote and gets exactly what it always got. The fused arm
    // steps over that slot with an extra `pc += 1`. Nothing has to be proved about jump targets and
    // no index moves, which is what keeps `Handler`, `IterNext`, `PushHandler` and `defs.sysl`'s
    // positional put-back all correct with no change at all.
    //
    // **THE SPAN IS THE FIRST INSTRUCTION'S, which is the one that can fault** in three of the four;
    // `AddStoreSlot` is the exception and reads the store's own span out of the slot it skipped, on
    // the path where the value is not a plain integer. A fault must name the character the unfused
    // pair named, and `tests_fused.sysl` is what says it does.

    // `LoadSlot(i)` then `LoadSlot(j)`.
    LoadSlot2(i: u32, j: u32)

    // `LoadSlot(i)` then `PushInt(v)` -- a local and a literal, which is most of what arithmetic
    // in a loop is made of.
    LoadSlotInt(i: u32, v: int)

    // `Add` then `StoreSlot(i)`. **Two whole numbers are written straight into the cell**, with no
    // push, no pop and no `keepable`: an `Int` is a value the absence rule can never refuse.
    AddStoreSlot(i: usize)

    // `Less` then `JumpIfFalse(to)` -- the shape every counted loop's condition ends in.
    LessJumpIfFalse(to: usize)

    // -- operators, ONE INSTRUCTION PER OPERATOR --
    //
    // **Which operator an expression writes is settled while it is compiled, so the machine must not
    // be asked again.** These were `UnaryOp(op: Tok)` and `BinaryOp(op: Tok)` until `per-op-instructions`,
    // and a `+` then cost three switches on that token -- the instruction, `on_values`, `arith` and
    // `int_arith` each matching it -- to reach an addition the emitter had already picked out.
    // CPython's `BINARY_OP_ADD_INT` is the same move. `op_instruction` and `unary_instruction` below
    // are the one place a token becomes one of these; `run_arith.sysl` holds what each one does.
    //
    // **The set is exactly what the parser can build**, which is `power_of`'s infix table minus the
    // three that short-circuit into jumps, and `prefix`'s three. An operator method, the set algebra
    // and the temporal operators need no instruction of their own -- they are pairs of values the
    // general arithmetic answers for, reached from whichever of these the program wrote.

    // Negation, truthiness and the bitwise complement: `-v`, `!v`, `~v`.
    Negate
    Not
    Complement

    // The arithmetic: `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `\`, `%`.
    Add
    Sub
    Mul
    Div
    IntDiv
    Rem

    // The comparisons. A chain of them is one comparison and still emits one of these per step.
    Less
    LessEq
    Greater
    GreaterEq
    Equal
    NotEqual

    // The bitwise operators and the two shifts.
    BitAnd
    BitOr
    BitXor
    ShiftLeft
    ShiftRight

    // **The token that is not an operator, which no program in the grammar reaches and which is here
    // so that a grammar that grew one would say so.** The token used to travel with the instruction,
    // so an operator nothing implemented arrived at `arith`'s last arm and was refused there by name;
    // with the operator picked at compile time that arm is unreachable, and a `_ -> Add` in its place
    // would answer an addition to an expression nobody wrote.
    NoOperator

    // -- containers --
    MakeArray(n: usize)

    // Takes `n` key/value pairs from the stack, keys first in each pair.
    MakeObject(n: usize)

    // Closes the object on top of the stack to writing, leaving it where it stood.
    //
    // **Only a `data` variant's constructor emits one.** A data value does not change, which is what
    // makes it safe to compare by what it holds and to use as a key in a table.
    SealData

    GetIndex
    SetIndex
    // `o.name` and `o.name = v`. **The second number is the site's own cache slot**, which is where
    // this instruction remembers the position a name was found at last time; `site_cache.sysl` says
    // what that buys and why remembering a position can never answer wrongly.
    GetField(k: u32, c: u32)
    SetField(k: u32, c: u32)

    // -- control --
    Jump(to: usize)
    JumpIfFalse(to: usize)

    // **The short-circuit pair leave the operand that decided.** `a || b` answers `a` when `a` is
    // truthy, which is slate's rule and not sysl's -- so these jump without popping.
    // `??`. Keeps the left operand and skips the right unless the left is null or absent.
    JumpIfPresentKeep(to: usize)

    // The inverse, which `?.` needs: jump where the value on top IS `null` or `undefined`, keeping
    // it for the arm on the other side to drop.
    JumpIfAbsentKeep(to: usize)

    JumpIfTrueKeep(to: usize)
    JumpIfFalseKeep(to: usize)

    Pop

    // `Pop`, for the value of a statement nobody asked for. **A separate opcode because only this one
    // means "discarded"** -- the stack is popped for a dozen reasons, and a duplicate popped off the
    // top of a compound assignment is not a value the program threw away.
    Discard

    Dup

    // Push a copy of the value `n` places below the top. `DupAt(0)` is `Dup`, which is kept as its
    // own instruction because it is by far the commonest.
    //
    // **This and `RotUp` are what a compound assignment needs.** `xs[i] += 1` must evaluate `xs` and
    // `i` once and then reach them twice -- to read the old value and to write the new one -- and a
    // stack machine has no other way to say that.
    DupAt(n: usize)

    // Move the top value down `n` places, so that `[a, b, c]` with `n` of two becomes `[c, a, b]`.
    // What needs it is `x++`, which has to leave the OLD value where the new one was computed.
    RotUp(n: usize)

    // -- scopes --
    //
    // A block gets one, and so does each turn of a loop: a closure made inside a loop body captures
    // that turn's scope and not the one before it.
    PushScope
    PopScope

    // -- functions --
    //
    // `f(a, b = 2)` -- jump where `b` was given, and otherwise fall through to the instructions that
    // work the default out and bind it.
    //
    // **The test is whether the name is bound in the call's OWN scope, which is what "was given"
    // means here.** A missing argument is not bound to anything: slate's `undefined` may not be
    // stored, so there is no value to bind and none to compare against -- the parameter simply is
    // not there. Looking outward through the chain would find a global of the same name and decide
    // the argument had been given.
    JumpIfBound(k: u32, to: u32)

    // The same question asked of a chunk whose parameters are slots: jump where parameter `n`
    // arrived, and otherwise fall through to the default.
    //
    // **ONLY A PARAMETER THAT CAN BE ABSENT IS GUARDED, and a slotted chunk with no evaluated default
    // has no head at all.** A parameter with nothing to bind but the absence itself -- a required one,
    // and a `b?` -- reads that absence out of the cell `lay_frame` padded, so the question is asked
    // exactly where an expression is waiting on the answer.
    //
    // **A slot has no way to be absent, so the frame carries a BITMASK of which parameters arrived**
    // and this tests one bit of it. The alternative -- an "unset" `Value` sitting in the slot -- is
    // refused on purpose: a sentinel is a thing that leaks into a program the one time the analysis
    // is wrong, and it would put a check on the front of every `LoadSlot` to catch it.
    //
    // **And a COUNT would have been wrong**, which is not obvious until you look at what a named
    // argument does. `arrange_named` builds its list with a hole in the MIDDLE -- `f(a, b = 1,
    // c = 2)` called `f(1, c: 5)` gives nothing for `b` and a value for `c` -- so "how many arrived"
    // cannot answer "did this one". One bit per parameter is the question actually being asked.
    JumpIfGiven(n: u32, to: u32)

    MakeClosure(chunk: usize)

    // A named function, which is a `val` holding a closure and is kept apart from one only so that
    // the name is in scope inside the body -- which is what makes it recursive.
    DefineFn(k: u32, chunk: u32)

    // `DefineFn` for a definition written at a file's own top level, which also writes the closure
    // into the machine's table at `d` so that `LoadDef` can read it.
    //
    // **The name still goes into the scope**, because a module's names are what an import reads and
    // what a `LoadName` the compiler could not resolve still has to find. The table is a second way
    // to the same closure and never the only one.
    //
    // `Unit.wide`: `a` the name, `b` the chunk, `c` the cell the closure is written to.
    DefineDef(w: u32)

    // **`names` IS AN INDEX INTO `arg_names`, AND ZERO MEANS EVERY ARGUMENT WAS POSITIONAL.** A
    // named argument cannot be placed while compiling -- which parameter `b = 1` fills is a question
    // about the callee, and the callee is a value nobody knows until the call happens -- so the
    // names travel in the instruction and `arrange_named` places them against the parameters the
    // function turned out to have.
    CallFn(argc: u32, names: u32)

    // `o.m(a)` -- look the name up on the object, and hand the object over where it came from a
    // proto.
    //
    // **A single instruction because the receiver rule needs both halves at once.** A `GetField`
    // followed by a `CallFn` has forgotten, by the time it calls, whether the function it is holding
    // was the object's own or a shared one delegated to -- and that is exactly the question that
    // decides whether an object is passed. `o.m` on its own still compiles to a plain `GetField`,
    // which hands back the bare function: taking a method off an object and calling it later is
    // allowed, and what you get is what you took.
    //
    // **`c` is this site's cache slot**, exactly as a `GetField`'s is: the lookup half of a method
    // call is the same walk a field read makes, so it remembers the same two positions.
    //
    // **The argument count is inline because it finds the receiver**, and `Unit.wide` holds the
    // three the lookup wants: `a` the method's name, `b` the written argument names, `c` this
    // site's cache. One load, inside an instruction that is about to lay a frame.
    CallMethod(argc: u32, w: u32)

    // `f(...xs)` and `o.m(...xs)` -- a call whose arguments were worked out while running.
    //
    // **The arguments arrive as one array rather than as a count**, which is the whole of what makes
    // them dynamic: `CallFn` carries how many there are in the instruction, and the number is not
    // known until the spread's array is in hand. `JoinArrays` is what builds it, so an argument list
    // may mix written arguments with spread ones in any order.
    CallSpread
    CallMethodSpread(k: usize)

    // Take the top `n` arrays and make one of them, which is how a spread argument list is built.
    //
    // **Anything that is not an array is refused here**, and the message names the spread rather
    // than the call -- `f(...42)` is a mistake about what may be spread, not about what `f` takes.
    JoinArrays(n: usize)
    Ret

    // -- suspension --
    //
    // **The instruction the whole machine exists for.** It takes the promise on top of the stack,
    // writes the frame it is standing in back so that there is something to come back to, and sets
    // the entire line of execution aside -- operand stack, frames, scope and all -- into `parked`.
    // The value it answers is written over the promise when the loop puts the machine back.
    Await

    // `yield e` -- hand the value on the stack to whoever is driving this generator, and stand still.
    //
    // **It does NOT pop, exactly as `Await` does not.** The yielded value stays in its slot while the
    // machine is set aside -- which is what keeps it reachable, the machine's stack being a root the
    // tracer already walks -- and the value the driver sends in is written over that same slot.
    Yield

    // Render the top `n` values as `print` would and join them, which is what an interpolated
    // string is.
    Join(n: usize)

    // `[v; n]` -- an array of `n` copies of the value under the count.
    Repeat

    // `base with { ... }` -- a copy of an object with `n` key/value pairs written over it.
    WithObject(n: usize)

    // `base with other` -- a copy of the base with everything the other object holds written over it.
    WithMerge(spread: bool)

    // `a..b` and `a..b by k` -- build a range from the ends on the stack, or from none.
    // `has_lo`/`has_hi` say which ends were written, since a range with an end left out takes it from
    // what it is used on, and `has_step` says whether a `by` left a third number above them.
    MakeRange(inclusive: bool, has_lo: bool, has_hi: bool, has_step: bool)

    // `x is Pat` -- test the pattern against the value on top, replacing it with the answer. Unlike
    // `TestPat` it consumes its subject, an `is` being an expression rather than an arm's guard.
    IsPat(k: u32, negated: bool)

    // `val [a, b] = xs` -- take the value apart and bind what the pattern names.
    //
    // **It consumes the value and refuses a shape that does not fit**, which is the difference
    // between a binding and a `match` arm: an arm that does not take simply lets the next one try,
    // and a binding has no next one. `immutable` is whether the names it introduces may be
    // assigned to afterwards, which is the whole of the difference between `val` and `var` here.
    Unpack(k: u32, immutable: bool)

    // The same, where this chunk's names are numbered slots. The pattern's names were claimed in
    // `pattern_names` order beginning at `first`, so the values the walk gathers go into
    // `first`, `first + 1`, ... with nothing to look up and nothing to allocate.
    //
    // **`immutable` is gone rather than carried**, a slot's writability being a compile-time fact
    // the emitter already recorded.
    // `masked` says the pattern carries defaults, in which case this leaves a BITMASK on the stack
    // saying which names the subject actually supplied -- one bit per name, in the same order the
    // cells are numbered. The guarded assignments that follow read it and a `Pop` takes it off.
    //
    // `Unit.wide`: `a` the pattern, `b` the first of the slots it binds.
    UnpackSlots(w: u32, masked: bool)

    // The same again for the shape a binding most often has: an array or an object whose parts are
    // all BARE NAMES, closed, with no `?` and no rest. `object` says which of the two.
    //
    // **Such a pattern cannot half-match**, every part being a name and a name taking whatever
    // stands in its place -- so the only questions are the subject's kind and its length or its
    // keys, and the cells are written straight out of the subject with the matcher never entered.
    // `unpack_op` in `match.sysl` decides which of the two instructions a site gets and
    // `unpacked_fixed` beside it is the half that does the work.
    //
    // **A name may carry a default, and `masked` is then `UnpackSlots`' own**: a name the subject
    // did not supply leaves its cell alone and its bit zero, and the same guarded assignments read
    // the same mask -- so a default is worked out where it always was, whatever expression it is.
    //
    // `Unit.wide`: `a` the pattern, `b` the first of the slots, `c` how many names it binds.
    UnpackFixed(w: u32, object: bool, masked: bool)

    // -- matching --
    //
    // Tests the pattern against the value on top, in the current scope, and pushes whether it took.
    // The subject stays put, because a failed arm is followed by another that wants it.
    TestPat(k: usize)

    // The same, where the arm's names are numbered slots beginning at `first`. **The names are
    // written only where the pattern took**, so an arm that misses leaves the cells of the arm
    // before it alone -- which is what its own scope used to do.
    TestSlots(k: u32, first: u32)

    // Jump where bit `n` of the mask on top of the stack is set, which is what a binding pattern's
    // defaults are guarded by. **`JumpIfBound` asked a scope and `JumpIfGiven` asks the frame**; this
    // asks the site's own mask, there being one per binding rather than one per call.
    JumpIfSet(n: u32, to: u32)

    // -- iteration --
    //
    // `for x in e`. The subject is on the stack under a position that `IterNext` keeps.
    IterInit
    IterNext(to: usize)

    // The guard in front of a `for await`'s `next()`, and it exists for the DIAGNOSTIC alone.
    //
    // **`for await x in [1, 2]` would otherwise draw *"`next` is not something an array can do"***,
    // which is true and sends the reader to look at `next` -- a name they did not write. What is
    // wrong is the `await`, an array being something a plain `for` walks, and this is what says so.
    // It answers nothing and takes nothing: the subject stays where the call is about to find it.
    IterAwaitCheck

    // Stop with a fault.
    FailWith(k: usize)

    // Stop with a fault that ends by naming the value on top of the stack.
    //
    // **`CheckResult`'s sentence with the test written out**, which is what a postcondition needs: a
    // clause's condition is an expression rather than a pattern, so what refuses it is an ordinary
    // jump -- and a message interned at compile time cannot say what the function actually answered.
    // A peek rather than a pop, for `CheckResult`'s reason.
    FailShowing(k: usize)

    // A `match` that ran out of arms, which names the subject it was given -- so the message has to
    // be built where the subject is, rather than interned at compile time.
    FailNoArm

    // -- types --
    //
    // `-> type` on a definition: test what the body left on the stack, and leave it there. **A peek
    // rather than a pop**, the value being the function's answer and not this instruction's.
    CheckResult(type_name: u32, pat: u32)

    //
    // `p: Point` -- test the named binding against a pattern and stop if it does not hold.
    //
    // **An instruction of its own so the complaint can name all three things a reader needs**: the
    // parameter, the type it was declared as, and what it actually got. A `JumpIfFalse` over a
    // `FailWith` would have had only a message interned at compile time, which cannot say what
    // arrived.
    //
    // `Unit.wide`: `a` the name, `b` the type it was declared as, `c` the pattern.
    CheckType(w: u32)

    // -- tests --
    //
    // Take the function on top of the stack and remember it as a test under the name it was given.
    //
    // **Only the file `slate test` was pointed at registers its tests**, which is what keeps a test
    // file that imports the module it tests from running that module's tests a second time. Walking
    // a directory runs every file as an entry in turn, so each file's tests run exactly once.
    DefineTest(k: usize)

    // Take the function on top of the stack and remember it as what runs around this file's tests:
    // `@setup`, `@teardown`, `@setupAll` or `@teardownAll`, which `kind` says.
    //
    // **One instruction for the four, because nothing but the moment they are called differs.** They
    // are registered where the tests are, by the file `slate test` was pointed at and no other, so a
    // test file that imports the module it tests does not inherit that module's `@setup`.
    DefineHook(kind: u32, k: u32)

    // -- handling a fault --
    //
    // **The handler is a place to STOP an unwind, and the unwind itself already existed.** A fault
    // leaves the instruction loop as an `Err` and has since the machine was written; what `try` adds
    // is a record of where to put the machine back to -- the operand height, the frame depth, the
    // scope, and the instruction the `catch` begins at -- so that the loop can be re-entered there
    // rather than handing the fault to whoever called it.
    PushHandler(to: usize)

    // The `try` body finished without faulting, so its handler is no longer owed anything.
    PopHandler

    // -- disposing a resource --
    //
    // **The value a `using` binds is refused HERE, where it is bound, and not where it is
    // disposed.** A resource that cannot be released is a mistake in the line that acquired it, and
    // the block it guards has not run yet -- so the reader is told at the declaration rather than
    // after the work is done. `null` passes, which is what lets an optional resource be written
    // without a branch around the whole block.
    WantDisposable

    // A handler that DISPOSES and lets the fault carry on, where `PushHandler` stops it.
    //
    // **The whole difference is that no fault object is built.** A `catch` turns the signal into a
    // value the program reads; a disposal has nothing to read it with and must leave the fault
    // exactly as it was, line and file included -- so `guarded` runs this handler's instructions and
    // then raises the very signal it was given.
    PushDispose(to: usize)

    // The end of a disposal handler's instructions, which hands the machine back to `guarded`.
    //
    // **Not `Ret`**: the stretch of code this ends is in the middle of a chunk and belongs to no
    // call, so there is no frame to pop and no caller to answer.
    DisposeDone

    // `throw v` -- raise a fault carrying what is on top of the stack.
    //
    // **The unwind is the one the machine already had**, so this op does nothing new: it turns a
    // value into the `Err` that every other fault leaves as, and `guarded` finds the handler exactly
    // as it does for a division by zero.
    Raise

    // -- modules --
    //
    // Push what another file exported, which is an ordinary object built by that file's own chunk
    // and kept in `module_values` under the index this instruction carries.
    //
    // **This is the only instruction that knows modules exist, and it knows nothing about files.**
    // Resolving a path, reading it, parsing it and refusing a cycle all happen while compiling; what
    // reaches the machine is a number. That is what "the VM never sees an import" means, and it is
    // what stops `import` from needing either a blocking read in the middle of an expression or a
    // promise that would force every importing program to be `async`.
    PushModule(m: usize)
end Op

// An instruction and where it came from.
//
// **THIRTY-TWO BYTES, AND THE WIDTH IS THE POINT.** The dispatch head loads a whole `Ins` before it
// knows which arm wants what, so what an instruction weighs is a tax on every instruction a program
// executes. At fifty-six it was a multiply by a constant that is not a power of two, four loads into
// six registers of a loop that had none to spare, and a stride that filled a cache line every three
// and a half instructions. Half of that came from `Op`, and `Op` is now sixteen: **no variant's
// payload is wider than eight bytes**, two operands being a pair of `u32` -- which is every index
// this program has, a string, a chunk, a slot, a jump, none of them near four thousand million --
// and the six instructions that genuinely carry three or four operands holding a `u32` into
// `Unit.wide` instead. The eight bytes beside a payload are the tag's, `PushInt` and `PushReal`
// carrying a whole 64-bit literal being what makes the enum eight-aligned.
//
// **THE SPAN STAYS HERE, AND IT WAS MEASURED BOTH WAYS.** A machine has no tree to ask afterwards,
// so every instruction carries where it came from -- and moving the spans into a buffer beside the
// code, read as `span_base + pc` only by the arms that can fault, measured **1.65% SLOWER** across
// `bench/` than leaving them in the instruction. The reason is that the arms which want a span are
// the arms that do the work: every arithmetic operator, every field access, every call passes one to
// the leaf it calls, and a call passes one PER ARGUMENT. Fetched with the instruction a span costs
// nothing at the arm; fetched from a second buffer it is an address, a bounds check and a load,
// paid on the hot path to save it on the cold one. `bench/results/2026-09-23-narrow-ins.md` has the
// table.
struct Ins
    op: Op
    at: Span

@assert(sizeof(Ins) == 32, "an instruction is two `ldp`: no payload past eight bytes, and a span")

// One function's code: the top level is a chunk, and so is every lambda and every named function.
struct Chunk
    code: Buf[Ins]
    params: Buf[string]

    // How many arguments a call must give, which is every parameter that has no default.
    //
    // **A count rather than the defaults themselves, because the defaults are already CODE.** They
    // are compiled into the head of this chunk, so nothing outside a call has to evaluate one; what
    // a caller needs to know is only where the arity it is allowed begins.
    required: usize

    // Whether the LAST parameter gathers what is left over -- `f(a, ...rest)`.
    //
    // **The name in `params` is the plain one**, the mark `ast.sysl` describes having been taken off
    // while this chunk was made. So a body binds `rest` and nothing downstream of compilation has to
    // know the spelling.
    //
    // A variadic call has no upper arity: `required` is still the floor, and the gather is what puts
    // the surplus somewhere.
    variadic: bool

    // What a fault calls it, and what a `<function>` prints as.
    name: string

    // Whether calling this runs it on a line of execution of its own and answers a promise.
    is_async: bool

    // Whether this chunk's body holds a `yield`, which is what makes calling it hand back a
    // generator rather than running it.
    is_gen: bool

    // How many cells this chunk reserves at entry: its parameters, then its locals, or only its
    // locals where it keeps its scope. Zero for a chunk whose names all stayed in the scope chain,
    // which is every module and any function every one of whose names something outside it reads.
    slots: usize

    // Whether this chunk's names -- its parameters as well as its locals -- are those cells.
    //
    // **A chunk that slots needs no `EnvObj` of its own at all**, which is what this exists to say
    // at the four places a call enters one. `vm.scope` becomes the closure's own captured scope, so
    // free names resolve exactly as they did and nothing is allocated per call. **Parameter `i` is
    // slot `i`**, claimed in order at the head of the chunk before a line of the body is emitted, so
    // no table has to travel from the compiler to the machine.
    //
    // **IT IS THE STRONGER OF TWO QUESTIONS AND `slots > 0` IS THE WEAKER ONE.** A chunk holding a
    // closure that reads one of its names keeps a scope for that name and puts every other one in a
    // cell, so it reserves a window and is not `slotted`. Anything deciding whether to build a scope
    // and bind arguments reads this; anything reserving the window reads `slots`. They were one
    // answer while a chunk either celled everything or nothing, and a mixed chunk is where they came
    // apart.
    slotted: bool

    // Which of the unit's sources this chunk was compiled from. **A fault's span is an offset into
    // this file and no other**, which is the whole reason a chunk has to remember it.
    source: usize

// Everything a compiled program is.
//
// **It lives outside the collected heap and holds counted boxes freely**, which the objects in the
// heap may not -- see `obj.sysl`. A chunk is immutable and acyclic once built, so reference counting
// frees it correctly and nothing here needs tracing.
struct Unit
    chunks: Buf[Chunk]
    strings: Buf[string]

    // How many characters each of those strings holds, in step with it.
    //
    // **A LITERAL IS COUNTED ONCE, WHEN IT IS INTERNED.** `PushStr` makes a fresh string cell every
    // time it runs, and a cell carries its character count -- so counting at the instruction would
    // walk the same text on every turn of a loop. The table is compiled once and read for ever.
    string_chars: Buf[usize]

    // The hash of each of those strings, in step with it.
    //
    // **A FIELD NAME IS HASHED ONCE, WHEN IT IS INTERNED, FOR THE REASON THE COUNT IS.** Hashing
    // walks the text, and a name written inside a loop would be walked on every turn -- so the three
    // instructions that look a name up in an object carry the answer with them instead.
    // `site_cache.sysl` is what reads it.
    string_hashes: Buf[u64]

    // Every whole literal in the program too wide for 64 bits, already built.
    //
    // **`PushBig` COPIES A VIEW WHERE `PushStr` READS TEXT**, which is the whole reason this holds
    // numbers rather than digits: a magnitude is built by dividing the text down a chunk at a time,
    // and a literal written inside a loop would pay for that on every turn. A `BigInt` copies with
    // a retain, so the instruction costs one.
    bigs: Buf[BigInt]

    patterns: Buf[Pattern]

    // The parameter names written at each call site that named any, with `""` for a slot that was
    // given positionally. **Entry zero is the empty list**, so a call that named nothing carries a
    // `names` of zero and no lookup happens at all.
    arg_names: Buf[Buf[string]]

    // Every file the unit was compiled from, in the order they were read. A `Chunk.source` and a
    // `Signal.Fail`'s `src` both index this.
    sources: Buf[Source]

    // The top-level chunk of each module, **in the order they have to run** -- every file before any
    // file that imports it, and the program the user named last.
    //
    // **This is not the order the files were read in**, and the difference matters: reading is
    // depth-first from the entry, so the entry is source zero, while running is post-order, so the
    // entry is the last module. A module's own index here is what `PushModule` carries.
    modules: Buf[usize]

    // The resolved path of each module, in step with `modules` -- which is how a compiled `import`
    // finds the number to put in its `PushModule`.
    module_paths: Buf[string]

    // The DECLARATIONS chunk of each module, in step with `modules`.
    //
    // **It holds the file's declarations and nothing else** -- its definitions, classes, data types,
    // types, externals and imports -- and answers the same exports object the module's own chunk
    // does, so a VM that has run only these knows every name the program declares and has done none
    // of what it does. `load_declarations` is the one caller and an ordinary run never enters it.
    module_decls: Buf[usize]

    // The packages the project reaches, so that a compiled `import ... from parsing` can be turned
    // into the same path the loader turned it into. **The loader's answer is not handed down as a
    // table**: there is one rule for what an import means and both passes ask it, which is what
    // stops the two from being able to disagree.
    packages: Packages

    // The top-level definitions of the file being compiled, and whether a read of each may still be
    // resolved. **Emptied between files** -- `defs.sysl` is the whole of what this is for.
    defs: Buf[ModuleDef]

    // Every resolved read emitted for the file being compiled, so that a spelling something further
    // down turns out to bind can be put back to a lookup.
    def_sites: Buf[DefSite]

    // How many cells of the machine's definition table have been handed out, for the whole unit. **A
    // number is never reused**, so two files' definitions of one spelling are two cells.
    def_total: usize

    // One cell per field-reading instruction in the program, holding where that instruction found
    // its name last time.
    //
    // **IT LIVES ON THE UNIT AND THE UNIT LIVES INSIDE THE `Vm`** (`vm_state.sysl`'s
    // `current_unit`), which is what makes a cache safe beside actors with nothing having to be
    // said about threads: an actor compiles its own declarations into its own unit on its own
    // thread, so no two lines of execution ever write one of these cells.
    site_caches: Buf[SiteCache]

    // The operands of the six instructions that carry more than a pair.
    //
    // **A PAYLOAD IS EIGHT BYTES AND THESE HAVE THREE OR FOUR OPERANDS**, so they hold a `u32` into
    // this table instead and read their operands from here. Every one of them is an instruction a
    // program runs rarely -- a declaration, an annotation check, a destructuring -- except
    // `CallMethod`, which spends the load inside a call it was about to make anyway.
    wide: Buf[WideOp]

    // `"proto"`, hashed once for the whole unit.
    //
    // **Every delegated lookup asks for it**, and a hash of five bytes computed at each one is a
    // real cost on a program whose methods live on a class. It is a fact about the language rather
    // than about a program, so it is worked out where the unit is made.
    proto_hash: u64

// The operands of one instruction too wide to hold its own.
//
// **THREE NUMBERS AND NO NAMES, BECAUSE THE SIX THAT USE IT MEAN SIX DIFFERENT THINGS BY THEM.**
// Each variant in `Op` says what its own `a`, `b` and `c` are, beside its declaration and at the arm
// that reads them; a struct per instruction would be six tables to keep and six things to intern
// where one does.
struct WideOp
    a: usize
    b: usize
    c: usize

// Where one instruction found a name last time.
//
// **A POSITION, NOT A VALUE, AND NOT AN OBJECT** -- which is the whole of why this needs no
// invalidation at all. A cell is a guess that is CHECKED before it is believed, by comparing the key
// standing at that position against the name being looked up, so an answer built from one is right
// by construction whatever has happened to the object, to its class, or to any other object the site
// has seen. A guess that has gone stale costs the search it was trying to save and nothing else.
struct SiteCache
    // Where the name sat in the receiver's own table.
    own: usize

    // Where `proto` sat in the receiver's table, and where the name sat in that proto's.
    hop: usize
    up: usize

    // The builtin a method call on a value of kind `kind` found last time (`method_kind_code`; `0`
    // is none). A call on a string, an array or a number asks a table rather than a proto chain, and
    // the table's answer is the same for every value of the kind.
    kind: u32
    native: NativeFn

// An empty unit.
//
// **There is no chunk zero.** Every module gets a chunk of its own and the program the user named is
// simply the last of them, so nothing is special about the first -- which is what stopped the entry
// from having to be both the first chunk and the last thing to run.
new_unit() -> Unit
    var u = Unit(buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), Packages(buf(), "", buf()), buf(), buf(), 0, buf(), buf(), "proto".hash())

    // Entry zero of `arg_names` is the empty list, which is what a `names` of zero means.
    u.arg_names.push(buf())

    // **Cell zero belongs to every caller that has no instruction of its own**, the machine reaching
    // a field read from a native or from an actor's delivery as well as from a `GetField`. It is a
    // guess like any other and is checked like any other, so sharing it costs those callers a stale
    // hit now and then and can never cost an answer.
    u.site_caches.push(SiteCache(0, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    u
end new_unit

// Record the parameter names one call site wrote, answering the index its instruction carries.
//
// **Not interned the way a string is.** A name list belongs to one call and is looked at once per
// call; searching the table for an equal one would cost more than the copy saves.
intern_arg_names(u: *Unit, names: Buf[string]) -> usize
    u.arg_names.push(names)
    u.arg_names.len() - 1

// Add a string to the table, reusing one that is already there.
//
// **Reuse matters more than it looks**: a field name appears once per access in the source and every
// one of them would otherwise be a separate copy in the unit.
intern_str(u: *Unit, s: string) -> usize
    for i in 0..<u.strings.len()
        if u.strings.at(i) == s then return i

    u.strings.push(s)
    u.string_chars.push(char_count(s))
    u.string_hashes.push(s.hash())
    u.strings.len() - 1
end intern_str

// A fresh cell for one instruction to remember where a name was found.
//
// **One per SITE and not one per name**, because what a site sees is one shape of object over and
// over where a name is shared by everything that ever writes it.
fresh_site(u: *Unit) -> usize
    u.site_caches.push(SiteCache(0, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    u.site_caches.len() - 1

// Write down the operands of one wide instruction, answering the index it carries.
//
// **Not deduplicated.** Two instructions with the same three operands are two instructions, and a
// scan of the table at each one would cost more than the twenty-four bytes it saves -- these are the
// rare instructions, which is why they are the ones paying the indirection.
intern_wide(u: *Unit, a: usize, b: usize, c: usize) -> u32
    u.wide.push(WideOp(a, b, c))
    u32(u.wide.len() - 1)

// A whole literal too wide for 64 bits, built once and kept.
//
// **The DIGITS are what is deduplicated**, two literals written the same way being one number, and
// a linear scan is what `intern_str` beside it does for the same reason: a program holds a handful
// of these at most.
intern_big(u: *Unit, digits: string) -> usize
    val n = big_parse(digits) match
        Some(v) -> v
        None -> big_zero()

    // **The comparison is of NUMBERS and not of text**, so `0xffffffffffffffff` and the same value
    // in decimal are one entry -- which is what a reader would expect of interning and is what the
    // string table gets for free from its keys already being canonical.
    for i in 0..<u.bigs.len()
        if big_cmp(u.bigs.at(i), n) == 0 then return i

    u.bigs.push(n)
    u.bigs.len() - 1
end intern_big

intern_pattern(u: *Unit, p: Pattern) -> usize
    u.patterns.push(p)
    u.patterns.len() - 1

// Which instruction an infix operator is.
//
// **ONE FUNCTION AND SEVEN CALLERS, which is what stops the two ends drifting.** A binary expression,
// a step of a comparison chain, `++`/`--`, a compound assignment, a compound multiple assignment and
// the two field and element forms of each all reach the same operator through different routes, and a
// second reading of the table anywhere would be a second thing to keep in step with the machine.
//
// **`AndAnd`, `OrOr` and `QQ` never arrive**: they short-circuit, so the compiler turns each into a
// jump that keeps the operand which decided, and nothing about them is an operator by the time there
// is an instruction to name. A token that is not an operator at all answers `Add` rather than
// faulting, the parser having refused it long before -- there is no expression in the grammar that
// reaches here with anything else.
//
// Not `private`: `compile_expr.sysl` and `compile_stmt.sysl` both emit operators.
op_instruction(op: Tok) -> Op = op match
    Plus -> Add
    Minus -> Sub
    Star -> Mul
    Slash -> Div
    Backslash -> IntDiv
    Percent -> Rem
    Lt -> Less
    LtEq -> LessEq
    Gt -> Greater
    GtEq -> GreaterEq
    EqEq -> Equal
    BangEq -> NotEqual
    Amp -> BitAnd
    Pipe -> BitOr
    Caret -> BitXor
    Shl -> ShiftLeft
    Shr -> ShiftRight
    _ -> NoOperator

// Which instruction a prefix operator is. `prefix` in `expr.sysl` reads exactly these three.
//
// Not `private`: `compile_expr.sysl` emits them.
unary_instruction(op: Tok) -> Op = op match
    Minus -> Negate
    Bang -> Not
    Tilde -> Complement
    _ -> NoOperator
